<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/periodic-table">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the 63 known elements by atomic weight and noticed the
    properties <em>repeated</em> — periodically. Lithium, sodium and potassium were all soft,
    water-hating-in-the-worst-way metals that formed similar salts; fluorine, chlorine and bromine
    were all volatile, corrosive non-metals. Instead of forcing the pattern, Mendeleev left gaps
    for undiscovered elements and predicted their properties in detail. When gallium (1875),
    scandium (1879) and germanium (1886) turned up on schedule — germanium matching his
    "eka-silicon" almost exactly — the table became chemistry's foundation. Today it runs to
    <strong>118 elements</strong> (the last four named in 2016), ordered by proton count rather
    than weight.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="electronegativity across the first 20 elements — the saw-tooth resets at every new shell" widget="plotter" config={enCfg} />

  <p>
    The zig-zag <em>is</em> the table's logic: electronegativity (an atom's pull on shared
    electrons) climbs across each period toward the halogens, then crashes back at the alkali
    metals where a new electron shell starts. Fluorine, at 3.98 on the Pauling scale, is the
    hungriest element of all — which is why it attacks even glass, and why it took chemists until
    1886 to isolate it, at the cost of several lives and careers. Notice also what the pattern
    implies about <em>why</em> the table works: the shape is set by electron shells filling and
    refilling, one period at a time.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="how to read the table in four steps" widget="equationStep" config={readCfg} />

  <h2>How the table is arranged</h2>
  <p>
    The modern table orders elements by <strong>proton number</strong> (atomic number) — the count
    of protons in the nucleus, which is the atom's true identity — and stacks them so that the
    outer-electron count repeats vertically. The horizontal <strong>periods</strong> (rows 1–7)
    correspond to electron shells: moving left to right, electrons fill the same outer shell
    (2 in period 1, then 8, then 8, then 18…), which is why the rows have those lengths. The
    vertical <strong>groups</strong> (columns) collect elements with the same number of outer
    (valence) electrons — and since the outer shell runs the chemistry, a group behaves like a
    family: group 1 metals all explode in water; group 17 elements all attack; group 18 gases
    react with almost nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Roughly 75% of the table is <strong>metals</strong>, sitting left of the bold staircase line:
    shiny, malleable, conductive. Right of the line, <strong>non-metals</strong> are dull,
    brittle when solid, and poor conductors. Elements astride the staircase — silicon, germanium —
    are the semiconductors your phone is built from, and their exact position on that line is why
    the electronics industry lives in one corner of chemistry. Reading the table fluently — group
    tells you the outer electrons, period tells you which shell — turns 118 separate facts into
    one system.
  </p>

  <h2>Group I: the alkali metals</h2>
  <p>
    Lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium (and radioactive francium) all carry a single
    outer electron, and they would rather do almost anything than keep it. The physical signature
    of that loose electron: these metals are <em>soft</em> (a knife slices sodium), <em>light</em>
    (lithium and sodium float on water), and have remarkably low melting points that fall further
    down the group — lithium melts at 181 °C, caesium at just 28.5 °C, so on a warm day caesium is
    a liquid metal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drop a pea-sized piece in water and the chemistry is theatrical: it skims the surface fizzing,
    releases hydrogen gas, and forms an alkaline hydroxide — 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. Lithium
    fizzes gently; sodium ignites with a yellow-orange flame; potassium ignites instantly with a
    lilac one; rubidium and caesium detonate on contact, shattering the dish. The trend is strict:
    <strong>reactivity increases down the group</strong>. The reason is pure geometry: each
    successive element has one more full shell between the nucleus and that outer electron, which
    shields the electron from the nucleus's pull — so it is lost ever more easily. This is also
    why alkali metals are stored under oil (to keep air and moisture off) and why none of them
    occurs free in nature: they live locked inside salts like NaCl.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the heavier the metal, the more reactive" is a decent rule for
  Group I — but students often learn the opposite because for many <em>other</em> metals
  (zinc vs copper, say) reactivity does fall with position. The Group I trend runs the same
  direction as the atomic size: bigger atom, more shielding, looser electron, fiercer reaction.
  And a second one: burning sodium does not make the water alkaline — the <em>hydroxide formed</em>
  does.</p>

  <h2>Group VII: the halogens</h2>
  <p>
    Fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine (and astatine) sit one electron short of a full shell —
    seven outer electrons — which makes them the strongest electron-grabbers in existence. They
    never occur as single atoms: each exists as a <strong>diatomic molecule</strong>, F₂, Cl₂,
    Br₂, I₂. Their states at room temperature descend the group from gas (fluorine, chlorine) to
    brooding red-brown liquid (bromine) to grey crystalline solid (iodine), and the boiling
    points behind that progression climb steadily:
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="halogen boiling points climb down the group — bigger molecules, stronger mutual attraction" widget="chart" config={halogensCfg} />

  <p>
    The rising trend has nothing to do with the strong F–F or Cl–F bonds; those are already
    broken. It is the <em>attraction between whole molecules</em>: more electrons per molecule
    means stronger temporary flickering charges and a stickier family. Reactivity, though, runs
    the <em>opposite</em> way from the alkali metals: <strong>it decreases down the group</strong>.
    Fluorine is violently reactive, chlorine fierce, bromine moderate, iodine tame. The reason:
    to react, a halogen must pull an electron <em>in</em>, and as the atom gains shells the
    incoming electron feels an increasingly shielded, distant nucleus. A displacement reaction
    shows the pecking order directly: bubble chlorine gas through colourless potassium bromide
    solution and the solution turns orange — chlorine has stolen the electron and displaced
    bromine (Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂), but bromine cannot displace chloride. Only the hungrier
    element wins.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the halogen trend is <em>not</em> the Group I trend. Alkali
  metals get more reactive down the group (losing an electron gets easier); halogens get less
  reactive down the group (gaining one gets harder). The two families bracket the table's
  appetite like a pair of jaws — group 1 giving electrons away, group 17 taking them.</p>

  <h2>Group VIII (0): the noble gases</h2>
  <p>
    Helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon sit at the table's right edge with <em>full</em>
    outer shells — helium's pair, the rest octets. With no electron to gain or lose, they have no
    motive: they are colourless, odourless monatomic gases that form essentially no compounds.
    That aloofness is their industrial virtue. Argon blankets molten steel and fills light bulbs
    because the hot filament cannot burn it; helium — far lighter than air and, unlike hydrogen,
    completely non-flammable — lifts balloons and cools MRI magnets; neon's reluctance to react
    is what makes neon signs possible, since a glowing, unreactive gas just keeps glowing. Radon,
    radioactive and heavy, is instead a hazard, seeping from granite into basements.
  </p>

  <h2>The transition elements</h2>
  <p>
    The squat block in the middle — iron, copper, zinc, chromium and friends — plays by its own
    rules. Unlike the alkali metals, transition elements are <strong>hard, dense, and high-melting</strong>
    (iron melts at 1,538 °C; tungsten at 3,422 °C, which is why it made the best filaments —
    though mercury, oddly, is liquid at room temperature). Their chemistry superpower is having
    several possible oxidation states, which shows up in names: iron can be iron(II) or iron(III),
    copper copper(I) or copper(II), each forming different compounds. Their ions are famously
    <strong>coloured</strong> — copper(II) blue, iron(II) pale green, iron(III) yellow-brown,
    permanganate deep purple — and their ability to hand electrons back and forth makes them
    superb <strong>catalysts</strong>: iron in the Haber process that makes fertiliser, nickel in
    margarine hydrogenation, platinum in your car's catalytic converter. Modern civilisation is
    disproportionately built on the middle of this table.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the table to make predictions</h2>
  <p>
    The table's real power is that it lets you <em>predict before you test</em> — the skill the
    exam actually rewards. Take two questions you should be able to answer without a
    laboratory. First: potassium sits directly under sodium in group 1, so it must have one
    outer electron like sodium's — but with two extra shells shielding it. Prediction: it
    reacts with water like sodium, only faster and hotter (it ignites with a lilac flame and
    skips across the surface). Second: chlorine and bromine both sit in group 7, bromine
    further down. Prediction: chlorine is the more reactive (its incoming electron feels less
    shielding), so chlorine displaces bromine from its salts but not the reverse. One
    position on the table → a quantitative statement about reactivity. The same logic
    extends to formulas: knowing barium is in group 2 (two outer electrons, Ba²⁺) and
    chlorine in group 7 (Cl⁻) lets you write BaCl₂ before any experiment, and knowing
    oxygen is group 6 (O²⁻) gives BaO.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trends also march in <em>size</em>. Atoms shrink as you cross a period — the nucleus
    gains protons that pull every electron harder while the new electrons pile into the same
    shell — and jump larger down a group, as whole new shells stack on. This single radius
    trend explains, in one move, why ionisation energy rises across a period and falls down a
    group, why metallic character (the ease of losing electrons) increases toward the
    bottom-left, and why the most greedy non-metals sit at the top-right — the diagonal that
    electronegativity's saw-tooth in fig. 1 traces. When two arguments collide, chemists
    say the element sits "on the diagonal": silicon and germanium, metal and non-metal at
    once, are the working proof.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> hydrogen's lonely position at the top of group 1 does not
  make it an alkali metal. It shares the single-outer-electron address but is a reactive
  <em>non-metal</em> gas that forms H⁺ — which is why some tables give it a column of its own
  (or three positions at once). Its placement is an argument, not a fact; the behaviour, not
  the column, is the chemistry.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Mendeleev's table had one quiet scandal: a few elements sat in the wrong order by weight —
    tellurium (127.6) before iodine (126.9) — which he fixed by trusting properties over mass and
    simply swapping them. He was right, but nobody could say <em>why</em> until 1913, when Henry
    Moseley measured the X-ray wavelengths of many elements and found they lined up with a
    single integer: the proton number. The table's true alphabet is the nucleus, not the weight,
    and Moseley's law even flagged the missing elements left as gaps — several of which he
    correctly predicted. He was killed at Gallipoli in 1915 at 27; scientists have wondered since
    what else he would have found.
  </p>
  <p>
    The table's bottom rows hide further architecture. The lanthanides and actinides are pulled
    out and parked below to keep the table printable, but they belong inside periods 6 and 7 —
    which is why the f-block is sometimes called chemistry's cellar. And the hunt for element 119
    continues at heavy-ion accelerators, where atoms are forged a nucleus at a time and survive
    for milliseconds. Theory predicts an "island of stability" of superheavy isotopes with
    unusually long lives; meanwhile, oganesson (element 118) is forecast to be a <em>reactive
    solid</em>, not a noble gas at all — the full outer shell stops protecting you once
    relativity distorts the inner electrons. Even the table's most dependable family may end in
    an exception.
  </p>
  <p>
    The families are also hidden infrastructure. Caesium's group-1 chemistry — one electron,
    flipped between two energy states by microwave light — is so precisely repeatable that the
    second itself is <em>defined</em> as 9,192,631,770 caesium oscillations; every GPS fix,
    stock trade and internet clock is disciplined by an alkali metal's electron. Gallium, a
    post-transition metal that melts in your palm (30 °C) and sits near the staircase, forms the
    compound semiconductors (GaAs, GaN) inside phone radios and the blue LEDs that won a Nobel
    Prize in 2014. Helium, the noble gas discovered in the Sun's spectrum (1868) before it was
    found on Earth, cools MRI magnets and quantum computers — and, being light enough to escape
    the atmosphere entirely, is the table's only element that regularly leaks off the planet.
    The columns are not just a filing system; they are the periodic table's supply-chain map.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mendeleev (1869); Moseley, "The High-Frequency Spectra of the Elements" (1913); IUPAC periodic table (118 elements); Pauling electronegativity scale; Cambridge IGCSE Chemistry 0625 syllabus (2026–2028), section 8.</p>
</TopicPage>
